  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon shows a potent trough across the \nwestern CONUS with broad southwesterly flow stretching from the \nDesert Southwest into the Northern Plains. An expansive area of low \nclouds remains in place across the region this afternoon, which has \nlimited heating and only expect a couple additional degrees of \nwarming. Forecast soundings show steep low-level lapse rates and \nweak instability this afternoon and evening, which could lead to the \ndevelopment of isolated showers. The aforementioned western trough \nadvances east tonight into Monday with a deepening low across the \nHigh Plains tightening the pressure gradient. Abundant cloud \ncover and strengthening boundary-layer winds will keep \ntemperatures mild overnight, with lows around 60. \n\nA lead shortwave ejects northeast across the Central Plains Monday \nmorning with guidance in decent agreement in this wave leading to a \nround of showers and storms that moves from west to east across the \nforecast area through early afternoon. Severe weather is not expected\nwith this round, although precipitation could bring gusty winds to\nthe surface. Stronger forcing comes through the day Monday as the\nlongwave trough approaches and low-level flow increases. Southerly\nwinds will increase to near advisory levels with gusts 35-40 MPH\nthrough the day. The progression of the initial round of precipitation\nwill impact when and where convection develops during the late \nafternoon and evening and how it evolves into the early overnight\nhours. Cloud cover is expected to hold across most of the forecast\narea with the most likely location for clearing and more substantial\ndestabilization across south central Kansas into Oklahoma, near\nthe dryline. Convective initiation is favored in this location \nduring the late afternoon before rapid upscale growth appears \nlikely given the strong forcing. Fast storm motions (40-50 MPH \nto the northeast) will bring this activity into northeast \nKansas fairly quickly during the evening hours, most likely as a\nline or broken line. Deep shear magnitudes are robust, but \nhodographs are rather disorganized with the most impressive \nshear limited to the lowest 2km. With that, considerable 0-1km \nstorm relative helicity values (250-300 m2/s2) will favor \nrotation in storms, either in embedded supercells or as \nmesovorticies along the leading edge of a line. Uncertainty \nremains regarding intensity of storms, but the strength of the \nlow-level shear does lead to the potential for damaging wind \ngusts and a couple of tornadoes. PWATs of 1.5-1.75\" supports \nefficient rainfall rates as well, but the progressive nature of \nstorms should limit the flooding potential. The most likely \ntimeframe for severe weather is 5-11 PM before the strongest \nstorms exit the area to the east. \n\nThe closed low is progged to slow in its eastward progression for \nTuesday, with wrap-around precipitation and cloud cover lingering \nthrough the day before the closed low pulls farther away. Another \ncompact shortwave follows closely behind on Thursday, but moisture \nmay be limited and leads to only low chances for precipitation. \nTemperatures become more seasonable Tuesday through the end of the \nweek with highs in the 60s and lows in the upper 30s to low 40s.",
